! ============================================================================
!  THE HERMIT  —  Frotzsmith sample (Inform 6 Standard Library)
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  Demonstrates a talking NPC. An "animate" object can carry a `life` routine
!  that responds to conversation actions (ask / tell / answer / give / ...).
!  Try:  north,  examine hermit,  ask hermit about woods,  give apple to hermit
! ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  KEY CONCEPTS IN THIS FILE
!  --------------------------
!  * Constant          — set the game title and subtitle strings
!  * Include "Parser"  — load the Standard Library parser (tokenises input)
!  * Include "VerbLib" — load the Standard Library verb handlers
!  * Include "Grammar" — load the grammar tables (MUST be last Include)
!  * [ Initialise; ]   — mandatory entry-point; sets `location` and prints
!                         the opening message
!  * Object / has light — room with the `light` attribute so it isn't dark
!  * n_to / s_to       — direction properties wiring rooms together
!  * Object -> (arrow) — places the object inside the parent (its location)
!  * Object x "..." Hut — places the object by NAMING its parent instead
!  * has animate       — marks this object as a living character
!  * life [ ]          — routine property handling conversation verbs on NPCs
!  * article           — overrides the "a"/"an" the library prints
!  * move x to y       — moves an object while the game runs
!
!  HOW THE GAME FLOWS
!    You start in a Forest Clearing. NORTH is the Hermit's Hut, where the hermit
!    sits and an apple lies. EXAMINE HERMIT, ASK HERMIT ABOUT anything, TAKE
!    APPLE and GIVE APPLE TO HERMIT: he accepts it. No ending.
!
!  MAP
!    Hermit's Hut   (hermit + red apple)
!         |
!    Forest Clearing   <- start
!
!  HOW A COMMAND REACHES THE HERMIT'S CODE
!    1. The parser reads GIVE APPLE TO HERMIT and matches it to a grammar line
!       for 'give', producing the action Give with noun = apple and
!       second = hermit.
!    2. The library's Give action checks the player holds the apple. If not,
!       the Standard Library takes it first ("(first taking the red apple)").
!    3. Because the hermit `has animate`, the library then asks the hermit's
!       `life` routine to deal with it. If `life` returns true, that is the
!       whole answer; if it returns false, the library prints its own stock
!       reply ("The old hermit doesn't seem interested.").
! ============================================================================

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  GAME METADATA
!  Constant declares a compile-time named value. Story and Headline are
!  special names the Standard Library prints in the "banner" at game start
!  (title, then subtitle). The `^` inside strings is a newline.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Constant Story "THE HERMIT";
Constant Headline "^A Frotzsmith sample showing a talking NPC.^";

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  STANDARD LIBRARY INCLUDES
!  The Standard Library is split across three files that MUST be included in
!  this exact order:
!    Include "Parser"  — the input tokeniser and object-matching machinery
!    Include "VerbLib" — action routines for built-in verbs (take, drop, etc.)
!    Include "Grammar" — grammar tables for those verbs; always goes LAST
!
!  PunyInform difference: PunyInform collapses all three into two files
!  (globals.h + puny.h), the second of which pulls in PunyInform's parser,
!  verbs and grammar in one go. It also commonly puts !% compiler-option
!  lines at the very top of the file. The Standard Library approach shown
!  here is the classic Inform 6 style and targets z5/z8 story files.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Include "Parser";
Include "VerbLib";

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  INITIALISE ROUTINE
!  The library calls Initialise() automatically at startup.
!  `location` is a global variable (declared by Parser) that holds the room
!  the player is currently in. You MUST set it here.
!  A bare string as the last statement prints it, adds a newline and returns
!  true. (Only a return value of 2 would change anything: it hides the
!  banner. So here the message prints, then the banner, then the first LOOK.)
!
!  PunyInform difference: instead of setting `location` inside Initialise,
!  PunyInform uses `Constant INITIAL_LOCATION_VALUE = RoomName;` before the
!  includes. The puny version of this sample prints its opening message with
!  `print`; a bare string would print just the same (that is the Inform 6
!  language, not the library), so the choice there is only style.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[ Initialise;
    location = Clearing;
    "^You set out to find the old hermit said to live deep in the woods.^";
];

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  ROOMS
!  Every room needs the `light` attribute; without it the room is pitch dark.
!  Direction properties (n_to, s_to, e_to, etc.) hold the destination room
!  or a routine. The library checks them when the player moves.
!  The Clearing names Hut before Hut is declared; Inform allows forward
!  references like this to objects.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Object Clearing "Forest Clearing"
  with description
        "Sunlight filters through the pines. A path leads north to a hut.",
       n_to Hut,          ! going north takes the player to Hut
  has  light;             ! `light` — room is lit; without this it is dark

!  The hut is the second room; its s_to leads back. Exits are one-way, so
!  each room lists its own.
Object Hut "Hermit's Hut"
  with description
        "A cramped hut, warm with woodsmoke. The clearing lies back south.",
       s_to Clearing,     ! going south leads back to Clearing
  has  light;

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  A TAKEABLE OBJECT — the apple, waiting in the hut
!  The `->` arrow in "Object -> apple" means the object is a child of the
!  most recently declared Object. Hut was declared last, so the apple
!  begins in the Hut, next to the hermit: the player finds it there and can
!  give it to him. (To start the player holding it instead, Initialise
!  would say `move apple to player;`.)
!
!  `name` property — a list of dictionary words that the parser accepts as
!  names for this object. Must be lowercase, single-quoted.
!
!  `has edible` — lets the library handle "eat apple" without extra code:
!  EAT removes the apple from play: "You eat the red apple. Not bad."
!  (Eat it and you can no longer give it away; the sample does not stop you.)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Object -> apple "red apple"
  with name 'red' 'apple',
       description "A crisp red apple.",
  has  edible;            ! `edible` — the player can eat this object

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  THE NPC — the hermit himself
!  "Object hermit ... Hut" places hermit inside Hut at game start.
!  Notice: no `->` here; instead the name after the short name names the
!  parent object. This form is handy when the parent is not the object
!  declared just before (here it happens to be the same Hut either way,
!  since the apple was declared with `->` and is not a room).
!
!  `name 'old' 'hermit' 'man'`
!    The words the player may use: HERMIT, OLD MAN, MAN all work.
!
!  `article "an"`
!    The article the library prints before the short name in lists such as
!    "You can see a red apple and an old hermit here." Without it the
!    library would print "a old hermit". (Any string works: "some", "the".)
!
!  `has animate`
!    Marks this as a living creature. The library will route conversation
!    commands (ASK, TELL, GIVE, SHOW, ANSWER, ...) to its `life` routine.
!    It also changes some stock replies: TAKE HERMIT answers "I don't
!    suppose the old hermit would care for that." instead of "Taken.", and
!    the hermit can be addressed with orders like HERMIT, HELLO.
!
!  `life [ ; ... ]`
!    A routine-valued property: the value in the property slot is a routine,
!    written inline between square brackets. The `;` right after `[` means
!    "no local variables". When a conversation action is directed at
!    an animate object, the library calls that object's `life` routine with
!    the global `action` set to the relevant action. The labels below
!    (`Ask, Tell, Answer:`) are action names; the routine body works like a
!    switch on `action`, and only the matching block runs.
!
!    Ask, Tell, Answer  — cover ASK HERMIT ABOUT X, TELL HERMIT ABOUT X,
!                         ANSWER X TO HERMIT. All map to the same response.
!                         An order the hermit doesn't understand, such as
!                         HERMIT, HELLO, also arrives here as Answer.
!    Give               — fired by GIVE <object> TO HERMIT. `noun` holds the
!                         object being given (`second` is the hermit). We
!                         check if it's the apple.
!    default            — any other life-style action. In the Standard
!                         Library those are Show, Kiss, Attack, ThrowAt,
!                         WakeOther and Order (an order the hermit could
!                         obey, like HERMIT, GO NORTH): KISS HERMIT and
!                         ATTACK HERMIT land here.
!
!    Returning a string (bare "...") prints it and returns true, which
!    signals "action handled — don't print a default message."
!    Without the `default:` block, life would return false for KISS and the
!    library would print its own reply instead.
!
!  `move apple to hermit;`
!    Changes the object tree while the game runs: the apple's parent becomes
!    the hermit, so it leaves the player's inventory. (The hermit is not a
!    container, so the apple is now out of sight: EXAMINE APPLE afterwards
!    gives "You can't see any such thing.")
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Object hermit "old hermit" Hut
  with name 'old' 'hermit' 'man',
       article "an",      ! "an old hermit", not the library's default "a"
       description "Weathered and serene, the hermit watches you calmly.",
       life [;
           Ask, Tell, Answer:     ! handles: ASK/TELL/ANSWER ... TO hermit
               "~The woods know more than I ever shall,~ the hermit murmurs.";
           !  Inside strings `~` stands for a double quote (") character.
           Give:                  ! handles: GIVE <noun> TO hermit
               if (noun == apple) {
                   move apple to hermit;   ! transfers apple from player to hermit
                   "The hermit accepts the apple with a grateful bow.";
               }
               !  Reached only when noun is not the apple (the block above
               !  returned). The hermit refuses, and the object stays put.
               "The hermit gently declines.";
           default:               ! any other life action (SHOW, KISS, etc.)
               "The hermit only smiles.";
       ],
  has  animate;                   ! `animate` — required for `life` to be called

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!  GRAMMAR — must be the LAST include in the file
!  This sets up the parser tables for all built-in verbs ('ask', 'give',
!  'tell' ...). The library checks that VerbLib came first. A brand-new verb
!  of your own can be declared before or after this line; `Extend`, which
!  adds to one of the library's verbs, must come after it. (There is no
!  TALK TO verb in the Standard Library: try it and the parser says "That's
!  not a verb I recognise." A Verb directive could add one.)
!
!  Include "Exits" is a small Frotzsmith helper that prints "Obvious exits:"
!  after each room description.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Include "Exits";    ! Frotzsmith: auto-list obvious exits (see Exits.h)
Include "Grammar";
